<div class="meta">Lesson 0024 · Systems design · from PR #99 (#95–#98)</div>
<h1>One Shared Asset Pool: Collapsing Two Pipelines</h1>
<p>"Created characters don't look like the townsfolk" and "add more
hairstyles" arrived as two requests. The audit showed one root cause: NPCs and
the creator rendered from <em>two unrelated construction methods</em> — rigged
glTF pack models vs primitive composites — that shared nothing. No amount of
tuning either pool fixes that; the fix is choosing ONE pool and routing
everything through it.</p>

<h2>Diagnose before adding assets</h2>
<p>The tempting move was to add hairstyles to the creator's catalog and call
it done. But hair added there could <em>never</em> appear on a roaming NPC —
rigged models can't wear composite parts. When two features share a root
cause, fixing the structure once beats patching each symptom: after
unification, "expand hairstyles" became six data rows that both consumers
picked up with zero wiring.</p>

<h2>The dispatch hook was already there</h2>
<p>The render loop already chose per-NPC:</p>
<pre><code>if (customLooks.count(i))  drawCompositeCharacter(...)  // created chars
else                       drawCharacter(visual);        // rigged pack model</code></pre>
<p>Unification = widening the existing branch until the else disappears:
give <em>every</em> NPC a look (<code>npcLooks[i]</code>) and the second path
is never dispatched. No new render system — the hook one feature built
became the seam the next feature widened.</p>

<h2>Authored data with a derived fallback</h2>
<p>Ten townsfolk deserve art direction; future procedural NPCs can't require
it. The rule that serves both, in one function:</p>
<pre><code>CharacterLook lookForPersona(name, authored, whyFallback) {
    if (authored && lookIsValid(*authored)) return *authored;   // art-directed
    ...
    return randomizeLook(fnv1a(name));   // deterministic: same name, same face
}</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>Authored wins</strong> when valid — the cast is intentional
(the cop's cap + visor + slate reads as uniform without the Knight model).</li>
<li><strong>Fallback is derived, not random</strong>: hashing the name means
an unauthored NPC keeps the same face across runs — no save data needed.</li>
<li><strong>Stale data demotes, never kills</strong>: a look naming a removed
part logs why and falls back; the NPC always spawns.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Validation lives with the catalog, not the parser</h2>
<p><code>PersonaLoader</code> checks only <em>structure</em> (five items);
whether ids exist is checked by <code>lookForPersona</code> at spawn. So the
persona format doesn't depend on the parts catalog, and editing the catalog
can't retroactively invalidate persona <em>files</em> — worst case a look
falls back at runtime, loudly.</p>

<h2>The trade-off was priced, not hidden</h2>
<p>Unifying retired five rigged, skeletally-animated models in favor of
primitive composites: real fidelity lost, bought cohesion + customization +
shared hair. The brief's priority ("literally the same assets") decided it,
the loss is logged, and the off-ramp (a modular CC0 pack, XL) is documented
in the plan rather than forgotten.</p>

<div class="callout good"><strong>The general shape.</strong> When two
consumers need "the same" content, don't align two pools — make one pool and
give every consumer a resolver (<code>authored → derived fallback</code>)
into it. Growth (new hair) then reaches every consumer by construction, and
the roster test can assert the interesting property (authored, valid,
pairwise-distinct) at the data level.</div>
